WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.720
Bounds-safe C starts with keeping data and size information together.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:11.220
A pointer should travel with the number of valid elements or bytes it can read, and writable storage should travel with its capacity.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:14.480
When a function appends, it also needs the current count.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.980
These facts should be part of the API, not hidden assumptions.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.960
Validate before access.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:28.020
Check indexes before reading or writing, and check ranges before copying, appending, parsing, or advancing a cursor.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:34.800
Validate both the start offset and requested length, and avoid pointer arithmetic until the range has been confirmed.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:38.860
Failure behavior should be explicit and should leave program state trustworthy.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:44.520
Distinguish count, capacity, bytes, elements, and string terminators.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.580
A C string is a bounded character buffer plus a terminator rule.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.400
A dynamic buffer is a pointer plus count, capacity, ownership, and lifetime.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.700
A loop bound is a promise about every index the loop will use.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.200
Keeping those meanings separate prevents many common mistakes.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:10.500
Finally, use review, tests, compiler diagnostics, static analysis, assertions, and sanitizers as layered support.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:15.360
Tools catch defects earlier when APIs are clear and tests exercise boundaries.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:27.580
The long-term habit is simple but demanding: every time code indexes, copies, appends, parses, resizes, or treats bytes as a string, ask what bounds fact makes that operation safe.
